From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hero Siege Offline Toolkit <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] HkYYError: report each distinct error once, count
 repeats, and measure the cost

Why: Hero Siege (GameMaker YYC x64) raises errors through YYError that the runner catches and carries on from, by the hundred per session. Upstream HkYYError answers every one with a full report: std::stacktrace, a symbol for every frame, the Aurie module list and a log write - and GmResolveGameSymbolFromAddress rebuilds its whole symbol map (every script, every builtin function with one by-name runner lookup each, every builtin variable) once per game frame of every one of those stacktraces, in a Release|x64 configuration that compiles with optimisation disabled. The shipped build lags in play, and this is the structurally certain suspect. This patch keeps the diagnostics and removes the repetition: the symbol table is built once and kept (GmPrepareGameSymbolTable: local unique_ptr, published with InterlockedCompareExchangePointer, the loser frees its copy, at most 2 build attempts and each in a different report, never kept with zero usable scripts, builtin walk capped at 0x10000 slots and skipped when no functions array is known, m_Functions/m_Name null-checked). The functions array has no readable length, so the builtin walk always looks at one slot past the last entry at the least, and upstream's YkExtractFunctionEntry builds a std::string from whatever name pointer that slot holds; each slot is therefore judged from its own two pointers BEFORE its name is read (GameSymbolWalk.hpp): a referenced name must point into the game image and be terminated within 256 bytes and before the image ends, otherwise the walk stops at that slot and one line says so; a routine outside the image is left out of the table but does not end the walk. A fixed ledger of 32 messages plus one shared overflow slot gives the first sighting of a message one full report, written verbatim through the new CmWriteLogOutputRaw (no CmpParseVa pass, so no 4096-byte cut-off and no '%' re-expansion), and makes every repeat a count-only path - one pass over the text and at most 32 compares, no stacktrace, no symbol lookup, no file write, no heap for messages under 1 KB. Two messages are "the same" if they match after every run of decimal digits is folded to '#'; the summary's variants= counter reports how often the digits really differed. A record is marked reported only after the log stream accepted the report; a failed report is counted and retried once; the log is asked whether it can take a report (CmLogIsWritable) before one is built; a report whose builder never came back expires after 30 s and is counted as failed, and if its finish turns up after all it is counted (stale=, stale_written=), its time goes into the totals and, if it was written, its record is marked reported. The original YYError is called on every path, always last, and always as ("%s", text): upstream passes the expanded text in the format slot, which expands any '%' in it a second time against arguments that are not there. BANNER DECISION: the "YYToolkit is loaded..." banner is appended only on the call that really wrote a report; every other call hands the runner exactly the text it would get with no YYToolkit loaded. Known limitation that follows: a fatal error whose text was already reported shows no banner and gets no fresh stacktrace - report #N for that text plus the last summary line is the diagnostic. The trampoline is cached only once it is non-null, a thread_local guard forwards re-entrant calls at once, and the status of the YYError MmCreateHook, which upstream discards, is logged once.

Evidence: Read from source, NOT measured: upstream Hooks.cpp:158-261 builds the report on every call and looks the trampoline up by name every time; Generic.cpp:26-101 declares the symbol map locally and refills it on every call, called per frame from Hooks.cpp:212; Interface.cpp:13-22 copies a referenced m_Name into a std::string before its caller can test anything about the slot; Generic.cpp:216 decrements upper_bound() unconditionally (undefined when it is begin()); Hooks.cpp:251 passes the finished report to CmWriteLogOutput as a printf format and Console.cpp:14-18 replaces anything of 4096 bytes or more with "<string too long to print>"; Hooks.cpp:258-260 passes the expanded message as the format; Hooks.cpp:357-363 never tests the MmCreateHook(YYError) status; YYToolkit.vcxproj:143 has Optimization Disabled for Release|x64. Reported from the field, not re-measured here: about 142 full reports in about two minutes of play on the previously shipped build, game lagging but running; the recorded message is "Unable to find any instance for object index '257087'" and the game continues after it (ForgePact/docs/S10-special-content-notes.md:1245-1252 in the hub). Verified on the build host: Release|x64 builds with 0 errors and the same warnings as pristine (C4244 x2 in Generic.cpp/Hooks.cpp, MSB8029), Release|Win32 compiles too; in the built DLL each declared marker occurs, and "[hs] YYError summary: total=" and "[hs] YYError report cost" occur exactly once each, in the format string of their own line and nowhere else. YYToolkit/hs-tests/yyerror_ledger_test.cpp passes 94 checks (table fills then overflow slot still reports; the overflow slot does not re-report its last message after the interval, keeps "reported" per message, and defers to a report in flight; repeat takes the count-only path; failed report retried once then counted; lost report expires; a late finish is counted, timed and marks only its own record; MostFrequent; both text fallbacks through a heap stand-in that refuses to grow and a format vsnprintf cannot expand - MEASURED: the build host's UCRT returns -1 for "%ls" with an unconvertible wide character; '%' forwards byte-identical through a stub trampoline; bounded gate refuses instead of waiting; summary schedule) and YYToolkit/hs-tests/game_symbol_walk_test.cpp passes 36 (a slot of leftovers is refused without its name pointer being read - the pointer used cannot be read, so reading it would crash the run; name bounded by the image end and by the length limit; routine outside the image kept out of the table; the three script-refusal causes told apart; a walk that accepted nothing is flagged). Both include the shipped headers, not copies, and 20 mutations of those headers, each applied alone to a scratch copy, each make a run fail (among them: drop the same-as-last-overflow-report test, drop the overflow m_Reported reset, ignore a report in flight in the overflow path, invert MostFrequent, leave stale ticks out, mark a stale record regardless of Written, do not flag truncation, make the image end inclusive, do not bound the name read by the image end, report "all rejected" as "none returned"), while the unmutated headers pass. NOT verified, and what the first live run is for: what a report costs on Hero Siege and whether the reports are what makes the game lag (each report ends with a "[hs] YYError report cost" line: total, stacktrace capture, game symbols over game frames only, other symbols, symbol table build, module list, log write; HkPresent writes an "[hs] YYError summary: total=" line at most once per 30 s and only when a counter moved); what the slot after the last builtin holds on Hero Siege's runner, and whether every builtin name there lies in the game image (the "built in" line prints builtins=A accepted of S slots and what the walk ended on; a "builtin walk stopped at slot" line means a slot was refused); whether the message's number varies (variants=); whether the runner's YYError returns or unwinds through the hook (returned=, dropped=, runner_ms=); whether the runner prints each error to the console YYToolkit allocates; the cost of std::stacktrace_entry::description(); what MmGetHookTrampoline returns for a missing hook.

Fails-safe: ORDER: the builtin walk reads m_FunctionsArray, so in the series this patch applies after the functions-array candidate filter; it stays out of HkPreinitialize and stacks with the ExecuteIt patch in either order. If no functions array is known the builtin walk is skipped and the build line says so. A wrong array is no longer only bounded by the 0x10000-slot cap: a slot whose name pointer is outside the game image, or not terminated inside it, ends the walk unread with one "[hs] game symbol table: builtin walk stopped at slot N: <reason>" line, and a walk that read slots and accepted no symbol is called out in the build line as what the wrong functions array looks like. INHERITED and not fixed: the slot itself is still read unchecked, as upstream reads it; and if a real builtin name of some runner lay outside the image the walk would stop there and later builtin frames would be labelled with the nearest earlier symbol - the line names the slot. If the symbol table cannot be built, one line gives the reason and what was supplied (NT header unreadable; m_GetScriptData unknown; no slot returned a script; scripts returned but 0 usable; C++ exception - with script_slots_walked, the first GetScriptData status, scripts_returned and scripts_rejected), the report is still written with the C++ runtime's frame descriptions, one more attempt is made in a later report, then one line says it was given up. Ledger synchronisation is a bounded spin-only gate, never an OS lock, so nothing can be orphaned at process exit into a hang: a caller that cannot get in takes the count-only path and is counted (busy=); if the gate stays shut for 5 summary checks in a row one "[hs] YYError summary unavailable" line says so and checks drop to one per second. If the log cannot take a report (never opened, or an error latched) the report is not built at all, the attempt is counted under report_failures= and the message is given up after the usual two attempts - no line can say so, there being no log. If a report throws or the log stream refuses it, one line says so, report_failures= moves and the message is retried once. With the ledger full a new message still gets a report from the overflow slot, at most one per 30 s. If the runner's text had to be cut (heap refused) or its format could not be expanded, one line per kind says so once and truncated=/unexpandable= count them. If Aurie has no trampoline the error is counted (dropped=) and dropped with one line (upstream would call a null pointer) and the lookup is retried on the next error. No diagnostic failure can stop the forward: everything before it is inside try/catch, and no gate is held across a runner call, a report, a log write or the trampoline. A wild read is not a C++ exception in this /EHsc build, which is why the slot check exists instead of a catch. No flag file, no console change, no lock in Console.cpp: logging stays as thread-unsafe as upstream (inherited, stated in a comment). The summary is written from HkPresent only, so errors in the last 30 s before exit may never be summarised.

Log-markers: "[hs] YYError summary: total="
Log-markers: "[hs] YYError report cost"
Log-markers: "[hs] YYError hook install"
Log-markers: "[hs] game symbol table: built in"
Log-markers: "[hs] game symbol table: build attempt"
Log-markers: "[hs] game symbol table: builtin walk stopped at slot"
Log-markers: "[hs] game symbol table: given up"
Log-markers: "[hs] YYError summary unavailable"
Log-markers: "[hs] YYError report #"
Log-markers: "[hs] YYError: Aurie returned no trampoline"
Log-markers: "[hs] YYError message truncated"
Log-markers: "[hs] YYError message not expanded"

Upstream-status: not submitted. Upstream v4.0.1 (5a95e46) has the per-call report, the per-frame symbol map rebuild, the unchecked name read in the builtin walk, the format-slot forward and the unchecked begin() decrement described above.

